+# ADALM-Pluto output
+
+Select **GPS Simulator → Settings → Output Sink → ADALM-Pluto**. The output
+works with Dynamic, Static and Interactive modes. HackRF remains the default;
+IQ File, UDP, TCP and Null keep their existing implementations.
+
+Use GNSS simulation only in an authorised, contained test setup. Follow the
+project's shielded-enclosure/direct-cable guidance. No hardware was used to
+validate this implementation; uninterrupted RF output and receiver acquisition
+still need a bench test.
+
+## Dependencies and build
+
+The application remains Rust-only to build: there are **no new Cargo dependencies,
+native link requirements or Cargo feature flags**. Pluto is an optional runtime
+dependency. Its Python helper is embedded in the executable, so there is no
+separate script to copy next to a release binary.
+
+Install Python 3.10 or newer, NumPy, Analog Devices' `pyadi-iio` and the native
+libiio library. `pyadi-iio` installs the Python `pylibiio` bindings, but pip does
+not install the native libiio DLL/shared library. Do not install the unrelated
+PyPI package named `iio`.
+
+```sh
+python -m venv .venv-pluto
+# Windows:
+.venv-pluto\Scripts\python.exe -m pip install -r requirements-pluto.txt
+# Linux/macOS:
+.venv-pluto/bin/python -m pip install -r requirements-pluto.txt
+```
+
+On Windows, use an Analog Devices libiio release matching your Python
+architecture and make its DLL directory available to that Python installation.
+On Linux, install your distribution's libiio runtime/utilities, or build the ADI
+release. On macOS, install libiio with your package manager or build it. Use a
+compatible native library and Python bindings; mixing libiio major versions can
+fail during import. A conda environment containing `pyadi-iio` is another option.
+
+Check dependencies using the **same Python executable** you enter in the GUI:
+
+```sh
+python -c "import numpy, iio, adi; print(adi.__version__)"
+```
+
+See the official [ADI installation guide](https://analogdevicesinc.github.io/pyadi-iio/guides/quick.html),
+[libiio releases](https://github.com/analogdevicesinc/libiio/releases), and
+[buffer documentation](https://analogdevicesinc.github.io/pyadi-iio/buffers/index.html).
+
+## Settings
+
+| Setting | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| Device URI | Explicit libiio device selection, default `ip:192.168.2.1`. A full URI such as `usb:1.2.5` is also accepted; use `iio_info -s` to find available contexts. Bare hostnames/IP addresses are prefixed with `ip:` for compatibility. |
+| Python executable | Executable name or full path into a virtual environment. No extra arguments or shell quoting. Defaults to `python` on Windows and `python3` elsewhere. |
+| Pluto TX gain | Hardware gain from -89 to 0 dB, default -80 dB. Negative values mean attenuation. This is separate from HackRF VGA gain and RF amp, which do not apply to Pluto. |
+| I/O timeout | 100–60000 ms, default 10000. Applies to libiio operations and the supervised helper. Startup allows an additional 20 seconds for imports and device configuration. Stop remains responsive while waiting for the helper. |
+| Sample rate | Shared RF setting, 1–20 MSPS. The same integer rate drives IQ generation and Pluto. Readback mismatch is an error. The upper bound is a configuration limit, not a throughput guarantee. |
+| Centre frequency | Shared RF setting. Pluto validates the stock AD9363 range (325–3800 MHz); firmware range extensions are not assumed. |
+| Baseband filter | Shared RF setting. If disabled, Pluto uses the sample rate as TX bandwidth; an explicit override must be 200 kHz–20 MHz. |
+
+Settings are locked while any simulation mode is running. Like the existing
+output selector, Pluto settings are session-only. The existing 3 MSPS GPS default
+is a reasonable starting point. Constellation/sample-rate requirements and the
+existing engine's modelling limits still apply; selecting Pluto does not add
+new GNSS models or resampling.
+
+## Public Rust API
+
+Use `SdrOutput::Pluto { config: PlutoConfig { ... } }` with
+`Simulator::builder().output(...)`. Both types are exported by `gps_sim`.
+`PlutoConfig::default()` supplies the GUI defaults. Set `uri`, `python`,
+`gain_db` and `timeout_ms` as needed. The existing
+`SdrOutput::PlutoSdr { host, gain_db }` variant now works as a compatibility
+shorthand with the default Python executable and timeout. Gain is negative dB,
+not HackRF's positive VGA setting.
+
+The historical builder methods `hackrf_sample_rate`, `hackrf_center_freq` and
+`hackrf_baseband_filter` also configure Pluto. Their names are retained so
+existing callers continue to compile. Without a rate override, Pluto uses the
+engine's constellation-based selection capped at the existing Pluto limit.
+
+## Implementation and errors
+
+The existing sc8 generator and FIFO are unchanged. Rust launches an embedded
+Python helper with an argument list (no shell) and sends fixed 262144-byte
+interleaved IQ blocks through stdin. The helper converts signed sc8 components
+to unnormalised complex values scaled by 256 for pyadi-iio's signed int16 TX
+input. It uses channel 0 and **non-cyclic** buffers: every push contains fresh
+samples, so dynamic routes do not replay a stale waveform.
+
+The helper acknowledges startup and each successful `tx()` call. Hardware
+back-pressure paces generation; no additional clock gate is added. Only one
+block is in flight through the supervisor, and the existing FIFO bounds queued
+generation. At natural EOF the helper allows four buffer durations to drain
+before destroying its TX buffer. Final IQ data includes the engine's existing
+zero-padded last block. Static loop passes reopen the backend, as existing
+hardware outputs do; seamless RF across pass boundaries is not guaranteed.
+
+Rust captures a bounded stderr tail for GUI errors, supervises stalled writes,
+and kills/reaps the helper on cancellation or timeout. The generator's FIFO is
+drained on TX failure so a blocked producer can stop. Python errors clean up
+the TX buffer in `finally`; forced termination relies on libiio/process resource
+cleanup. Missing Python/packages/DLLs, invalid settings, device-open errors and
+transfer failures do not silently fall back to another output.
+
+If imports fail, check the selected Python and native libiio installation. If
+opening the device fails, verify the URI and USB/network driver. If transfer
+timeouts occur, check host/USB performance and configured rate. No automatic
+USB discovery or driver installation is performed by the application.
+
+## Hardware-free checks
+
+```sh
+python -m pip install numpy
+python -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_pluto_bridge.py -v
+cargo fmt --all -- --check
+cargo check --workspace --all-targets --locked
+cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings -W clippy::all
+cargo test --workspace --all-targets --all-features
+cargo test --workspace --doc
+```
+
+Python tests use a fake radio to verify all signed sample values, I/Q order,
+partial pipe reads, multiple fresh buffers, radio settings, non-cyclic mode,
+dependency errors, sample-rate mismatch and cleanup after transfer failure.
+They never open a real Pluto. Rust unit tests check configuration and URI
+normalisation. The Python helper tests also run in CI on Windows, Linux and macOS.
+
+Before relying on this backend, validate on physical hardware in the contained
+test setup: connection for each supported URI type, measured RF rate/frequency,
+amplitude, sustained transfer, natural completion, Stop, disconnects and static
+loop transitions. Software tests do not prove RF continuity or a receiver fix.
